B. It should use position: fixed instead of absolute
C. z-index is missing, so another element pushes it away
D. An absolutely positioned box is placed relative to its nearest positioned ancestor; the card needs position: relative
Reference answer: D
For position:absolute, top/right/bottom/left are resolved against the nearest ancestor whose position is not static. If no such ancestor exists the box is placed against the initial containing block, which is the viewport — so the badge ends up pinned to the page corner. This is one of the most frequent CSS bugs there is. The fix is to add position: relative to the card; relative alone does not move the card because it has no offsets, it only establishes the reference frame. Switching to position: fixed would make it worse, since fixed always resolves against the viewport. z-index controls stacking order, not which box is used for positioning.
