Specificity compares four positions in order. #submit is (0,1,0,0) — one ID. .btn:hover is (0,0,2,0) — one class plus one pseudo-class. The ID position outranks the class position, so #submit always wins and the hover colour never applies. Best fix: convert the base rule to a class selector, .btn { ... }, taking IDs out of the stylesheet entirely. Renaming the hover rule to #submit:hover also works but keeps accumulating ID debt; !important works too but makes all later overrides fail and is a technical debt starter. Review signal: an ID selector in a stylesheet is itself worth flagging, because its weight makes every later class-based override fail. That is why BEM, CSS Modules and Tailwind all avoid IDs. The other options are wrong: :hover applies to any element, cursor only changes the pointer shape, and the background shorthand participates in the cascade normally.
